WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:03.258
Module Three, Lesson One: evaluating agents.

2
00:00:03.588 --> 00:00:05.095
What should you measure, and where?

3
00:00:05.895 --> 00:00:09.237
Picture a travel bot that books flights and hotels for an office.

4
00:00:09.567 --> 00:00:13.485
One Monday, its success rate drops from eighty-two percent to seventy.

5
00:00:14.285 --> 00:00:16.433
The team stares at that one number for a week.

6
00:00:16.763 --> 00:00:19.187
It says something is wrong, but nothing about where.

7
00:00:19.987 --> 00:00:25.143
Agent evaluation works in four layers: component, step, trajectory, and outcome.

8
00:00:25.473 --> 00:00:27.364
Each one answers a different question.

9
00:00:28.164 --> 00:00:31.805
Component checks test each piece alone, like retrieval recall.

10
00:00:32.135 --> 00:00:35.989
Step checks grade each decision, like the tool and arguments chosen.

11
00:00:36.789 --> 00:00:39.768
Trajectory checks score the whole path the agent took.

12
00:00:40.098 --> 00:00:44.274
Six redundant searches and a lucky guess can pass the outcome check,

13
00:00:44.324 --> 00:00:45.765
yet fail on reliability.

14
00:00:46.565 --> 00:00:49.459
Outcome checks ask whether the task actually got done.

15
00:00:49.789 --> 00:00:53.345
Think task success, groundedness, and cost per resolved task.

16
00:00:54.145 --> 00:00:57.615
For retrieval, four RAG metrics form a diagnostic grid.

17
00:00:57.945 --> 00:01:01.448
Low context recall means the evidence was never fetched;

18
00:01:01.498 --> 00:01:05.319
high recall with low faithfulness means the model ignored it.

19
00:01:06.119 --> 00:01:09.333
So test retrieval on its own, before the generator runs.

20
00:01:09.663 --> 00:01:11.752
That exposed the travel team's culprit:

21
00:01:11.802 --> 00:01:14.221
hotel search was fetching the wrong listings.

22
00:01:15.021 --> 00:01:17.531
A golden dataset keeps it from happening again.

23
00:01:17.861 --> 00:01:22.035
It's versioned with the code, drawn from real traffic, and full of hard cases.

24
00:01:22.835 --> 00:01:27.691
Source it from production traffic, synthetic data, and expert adversarial cases.

25
00:01:28.021 --> 00:01:31.619
Then run it in CI on every prompt, model, or tool change.

26
00:01:32.419 --> 00:01:35.356
Agents are stochastic, so one run proves nothing.

27
00:01:35.686 --> 00:01:38.225
Pass@k means any of k runs succeeded;

28
00:01:38.275 --> 00:01:42.143
pass^k means all of them did, and production needs that.

29
00:01:42.943 --> 00:01:45.879
A thirty-case suite swings about eighteen points either way.

30
00:01:46.209 --> 00:01:50.149
You need hundreds of cases before small gains justify a release.

31
00:01:50.949 --> 00:01:55.485
Releases then move from offline tests to shadow, canary, and full rollout.

32
00:01:55.815 --> 00:01:59.797
Shadow mirrors live traffic to the new version but never serves its answers.

33
00:02:00.597 --> 00:02:04.631
Exam tip: zero-risk validation on real traffic means shadow,

34
00:02:04.681 --> 00:02:07.353
since canary already exposes some users.

35
00:02:07.683 --> 00:02:09.595
Read on for every layer in detail.
